What your actual pain points are
You have three separate problems that feel like one:

Recall is unmeasured — you know 88.4% of what the pipeline calls positive is genuinely positive, but you do not know what fraction of all genuinely positive jobs the pipeline is finding
Re-evaluation is expensive — every parameter change requires repeating the manual exercise
Domain transfer is expensive — moving to green jobs feels like starting from scratch
These are solvable independently. Let me take each one.

Problem 1 — Calculating recall without a full labeled dataset
To calculate recall you need to know the false negative rate — jobs the pipeline missed that should have been included.

The brute force approach (sample 100 random negatives, manually check) works but is painful. Here is a smarter approach using what you already have.

The SOC anchor trick:

You already know which SOC occupation codes are your ground truth positives. Database administrators (SOC 3133) should be data intensive — that is why they are anchor occupations. Cleaners (SOC 9111) should not be.


Recall upper bound:
  Sample 100 jobs where SOC code = 3133 (database administrators)
  Run through pipeline
  Count how many are classified as data-intensive
  That % is your recall on the "obvious" positives
If your pipeline only captures 60% of database administrator jobs, that is a recall problem you need to fix. If it captures 90%+, your recall on true positives is healthy.

This costs you zero additional manual labeling because SOC codes are already in your data.

The negative sanity check:


Sample 100 jobs where SOC code is in clearly non-data occupations
(e.g., 9111 cleaners, 6141 nursing auxiliaries, 5310 construction workers)
Run through pipeline
Any classified as data-intensive = false positives from a different angle
Between these two checks you get bounds on both precision (from what you already have: 88.4%) and recall (from the anchor SOC sampling), without any new manual annotation.

Problem 2 — Avoiding repeated manual work when you change parameters
This is the most fixable problem and the answer is: treat the 100 labeled jobs as a permanent benchmark dataset.

Right now you are thinking of that 100-job exercise as a one-time measurement. Instead, save those 100 jobs with their manual labels as a CSV file and never touch the labels again:


job_id | full_text | manual_label (1/0/?) | notes
Every time you change a parameter — DATA_THRESHOLD from 3 to 5, REL_SHARE_THRESHOLD from 10 to 15, GOLD_STANDARD with a new word — you run the pipeline on just those 100 jobs and compare output against the manual labels. You get updated precision and recall in seconds, not weeks.

This is exactly how machine learning teams handle evaluation: you create a held-out test set once, label it once carefully, and then use it forever. The 100 jobs the reviewer coded are your test set. You do not need more.

The only time you need new labeling is when you move to a new domain — but even then, 50 labeled examples is usually enough for a benchmark (not 500+).

Problem 3 — Domain transfer to green jobs
For green jobs you would need a new benchmark set, but you do not need anywhere near as many as you might think for parameter tuning. Here is the minimum viable approach:

Run the pipeline on green jobs with default parameters
Sample 50 positives, have someone label them — takes maybe 2 hours
Sample 20 jobs from clearly green SOC codes (e.g., environmental scientists) — verifies recall
Those 70 labeled examples become your green jobs benchmark forever
The 50-job exercise is a one-time cost per domain, not a recurring cost. The recurring cost drops to zero once the benchmark exists.

The vertical offset — this is a separate problem from accuracy
You said results follow the same trend as historical manual results but with a systematic offset. That is actually good news — it means the pipeline is measuring the right thing, just with a different baseline.

A constant offset usually means one of three things:

Scope difference — your pipeline captures a broader or narrower definition of "data intensive" than the historical manual method used
Denominator difference — the historical figures may have used a different job universe (e.g., only full-time jobs, or only certain sectors)
Threshold calibration — DATA_THRESHOLD or REL_SHARE_THRESHOLD is set too high or low relative to the manual definition
You can diagnose which one it is without any new labeling: look at the offset by sector. If it is constant across all sectors, it is a calibration issue. If it varies, it is a scope or denominator issue.

What I would actually do in your position
In order of effort vs value:

Week 1 (no new labeling):

Save the 100 labeled jobs as a CSV benchmark file
Build a 20-line evaluation script that runs those 100 jobs through the pipeline and prints precision/recall against the manual labels
Run that script after every parameter change — this replaces all future manual re-evaluation for the data domain
Week 2 (1–2 hours of new labeling):

Sample 100 jobs from anchor SOC codes (3133, 2433, 3544) and run through pipeline — gives recall estimate on obvious positives
Sample 50 jobs from clearly non-data SOC codes and run through pipeline — gives false positive rate from a different angle
When moving to green jobs:

Sample 50 pipeline positives, label them once
Sample 20 from green SOC codes
Those 70 become the green jobs benchmark permanently
One question I have for you
You mentioned a "vertical offset" — is this offset roughly constant across years (e.g., your pipeline consistently reads 2–3 percentage points higher than the historical figures), or does the gap grow or shrink over time?

If it is constant, a simple scalar calibration correction is all you need and the relative comparisons across sectors and years are still valid. If it changes over time, that points to something structural in how the pipeline handles temporal variation which is worth diagnosing before publishing.


Correction 1 — the 500-job minimum threshold:
The dictionary is built on the full 62M corpus. A term only enters the dictionary if it appears in 500+ unique jobs. If you run the pipeline on a small sample of 100 jobs, no term will reach that threshold so the dictionary comes out empty. You cannot rebuild the dictionary on a small benchmark — it requires the full corpus every time any dictionary-level parameter changes.

Correction 2 — anchor SOC ≠ ground truth positive:
Even SOC 3133 (database administrators) will have job postings that are generic, poorly written, or administrative in nature and won't contain 3 vocabulary terms. So a database admin job posting is not a guaranteed true positive. The SOC code tells you the occupation — it says nothing about whether the specific job description mentions data skills explicitly enough to cross the threshold.

What this changes
My benchmark suggestion still works, but only for one specific case. Let me separate what is actually testable cheaply from what is not.

Parameters that only affect Phase 3 (classification) — cheap to test:

DATA_THRESHOLD is the only one. The dictionary is already built. You apply it to jobs. Changing DATA_THRESHOLD from 3 to 4 does not require rebuilding anything.

For this parameter only, your 100 labeled jobs work as a permanent benchmark. You do not re-run the full pipeline — you just re-apply the threshold to the already-classified unique_data_terms counts for those 100 jobs. Precision and recall against your manual labels update instantly.

Parameters that affect the dictionary — expensive, no shortcut:

REL_SHARE_THRESHOLD, global_count >= 500, SIM_GROUNDING, GOLD_STANDARD all change which terms enter the dictionary. Changing any of these requires a full pipeline run on 62M jobs before you can evaluate anything. There is no cheap way around this.

The recall problem given these constraints
The honest situation: true recall cannot be calculated without a labeled dataset, and you cannot feasibly create one large enough for a corpus-dependent pipeline like this. That is not a failure of your approach — it is a known limitation of semi-supervised pipelines built on large unlabeled corpora.

What you can do instead is estimate it indirectly through two approaches that do not require re-running the full pipeline:

Approach 1 — Near-threshold sampling (most efficient)

Your pipeline likely stores unique_data_terms per job during classification. The false negatives most likely to exist are jobs that scored 2 terms when the threshold is 3 — they almost made the cut.

Sample 50 jobs where unique_data_terms = DATA_THRESHOLD - 1. Have someone label them. If 70%+ of those are genuinely data-intensive, your threshold is excluding real positives and recall is suffering. If only 20% are genuine, your threshold is calibrated correctly and your recall on the meaningful cases is probably reasonable.

This is one 2-hour exercise, reusable for any threshold change, and it gives you directional recall evidence without needing a full labeled dataset.

Approach 2 — Dictionary audit (no job labeling required)

Instead of auditing jobs, audit the dictionary. Print the polished dictionary sorted by relative_share and have the same person who did your precision exercise spend one hour answering: are there obvious, well-known database/analytics skills that are missing from this list entirely?

If "ETL", "data lake", "data warehouse", "data pipeline" are absent, that is direct evidence of a recall problem at the vocabulary level — the pipeline never had a chance to find jobs mentioning those skills. This does not require touching the 62M records at all.

Missing terms in the dictionary = systematic false negatives in the classification.

The practical workflow going forward
Given all of this, here is what I would actually do:

Goal	Method	One-time cost	Recurring cost
Precision tracking	100-job benchmark CSV	Already done	Zero — re-evaluate automatically
DATA_THRESHOLD tuning	Apply threshold to stored unique_data_terms for benchmark jobs	Zero	Zero
Dictionary-level parameter changes	Full pipeline re-run + re-evaluate benchmark	Hours (pipeline)	Same — benchmark labels never change
