One question: does your pipeline currently export unique_data_terms per job alongside the binary is_data_intensive flag? If not, that is a one-line change worth making — it unlocks the near-threshold sampling without any extra computation.



What you actually have is a precision-only validation with an estimated figure of 88.4%. That is one number. It tells you that when the pipeline says a job is data-intensive, it is right about 88% of the time. That is genuinely useful and not nothing.

But for industry or academic standards you need at minimum:

Precision and recall, giving you an F1 score
A properly constructed held-out test set, labeled before you see the results (not after)
Inter-annotator agreement score (Cohen's Kappa) to show the labels themselves are reliable — your reviewer noted 5 borderline cases out of 100, which is a meaningful ambiguity rate
The test set should be stratified — representative across sectors, years, SOC codes — not just 100 random positives
What you fundamentally lack is a ground truth labeled dataset built independently of the pipeline output. Everything built on top of that gap is a workaround, not a solution. The near-threshold sampling and dictionary auditing I described are diagnostic tools — they help you improve the pipeline — but they do not produce publishable validation metrics.

The real options if you need this to be rigorous
Option 1 — Commission a proper annotation exercise

This is the industry standard answer. You would need:

A stratified random sample of perhaps 500–1000 jobs drawn from the full corpus, independent of pipeline output
Two or three independent annotators labeling each job
An adjudication process for disagreements
Inter-rater reliability reported alongside the results
This is expensive. It is also the only way to get a number that survives peer review or external scrutiny.

Option 2 — Reframe what the pipeline is and what it claims

If full ground truth labeling is not feasible, the intellectually honest move is to reframe the pipeline not as a classifier with a precision/recall claim, but as a measurement instrument with a known calibration. You report:

Precision of 88.4% on a sample of positives (with confidence interval)
Known failure modes from the false positive analysis (semantically similar skills, long descriptions, etc.)
Comparison to external benchmarks like ONS or OECD digital economy figures for face validity
The systematic offset explained as a definitional scope difference
This is what a lot of labour market research actually does. It is not claiming to be a machine learning classifier — it is claiming to be a measurement methodology with documented limitations. That is a legitimate and publishable framing if the limitations are stated clearly.

Option 3 — Find or create a proxy labeled dataset from an external source

Some organisations have done related work:

OECD has its own digital skills classifications across member countries
Lightcast (your data provider) may have their own skill taxonomy and tagged jobs
Academic papers on digital labour markets sometimes release annotated datasets
If any of these overlap with your job corpus you might be able to construct a test set without manual annotation.

The honest summary
The pipeline produces defensible and useful results. The 88.4% precision figure is real evidence. But if someone asked you in a formal review "what is your recall and how was your test set constructed", you currently do not have a satisfying answer. Whether that matters depends entirely on the audience and the purpose — internal policy work has a very different bar than a published methodology paper.

What is the actual end use of this? That would determine how much rigour is genuinely needed versus how much would be over-engineering for the context.
